opulated with
            high-level classes of the CIDOC CRM, particularly
            sub-classes of <a
            href="https://cidoc-crm.org/html/cidoc_crm_v7.1.1_with_translations.html#E18">E18
            Physical Thing</a>, there is now a <a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00092">Category
            Vocabulary</a> available from the LIDO Terminology for use
            in this element. Note that a &lt;lido:category&gt; statement
            must be logically and semantically consistent with what is
            said in the more specific &lt;lido:objectWorkType&gt; and
            &lt;lido:classification&gt; elements.</p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> In the following example, the
            category statement is fully expressed by the LIDO
            Terminology identifier, <a
            href="http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00096">“lido00096”</a>.
            The term in &lt;skos:prefLabel&gt; is given here both for
            human readability and for use by processing systems that are
            not prepared for fetching data using a vocabulary URI.</p>
            <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Category:</strong> Human-made object
            <strong><em>Preference:</em></strong> Preferred</p>
            </blockquote>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb8"><pre
            class="sourceCode xml"><code class="sourceCode xml"><span id="cb8-1"><a href="#cb8-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;<span class="kw">lido:category</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb8-2"><a href="#cb8-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span><span class="ot"> rdf:about=</span><span class="st">&quot;http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00096&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb8-3"><a href="#cb8-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>        &lt;<span class="kw">skos:prefLabel</span><span class="ot"> xml:lang=</span><span class="st">&quot;en&quot;</span>&gt;Human-made object&lt;/<span class="kw">skos:prefLabel</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb8-4"><a href="#cb8-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    &lt;/<span class="kw">skos:Concept</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb8-5"><a href="#cb8-5" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;/<span class="kw">lido:category</span>&gt;</span></code></pre></div>
            <h3 data-number="4.2.4" id="application-profile"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.2.4</span> Application
            Profile</h3>
            <p><strong>Element name:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#applicationProfile">lido:applicationProfile</a>&gt;</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> The LIDO schema may not cover all
            metadata needs of the user community. To accommodate
            specialized user requirements, the LIDO schema can be
            complemented with application profiles. Whenever an
            application profile has been used in the preparation of a
            LIDO record, the &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#applicationProfile">lido:applicationProfile</a>&gt;
            element should carry the public identifier (URI or
            equivalent) of the respective document(s). For further
            information on LIDO profiles, see section <a
            href="#application-profiles"><em>3.4.3 Application
            Profiles</em></a> and <a
            href="https://cidoc.mini.icom.museum/working-groups/lido/lido-overview/profiles/"
            class="uri">https://cidoc.mini.icom.museum/working-groups/lido/lido-overview/profiles/</a>.</p>
            <p><strong>Example:</strong> In the following example the
            Application Profile Element contains the URI of the XML
            Schema Definition Document of the <a
            href="https://doi.org/10.11588/arthistoricum.1026">LIDO
            Application Profile Painting and Sculpture</a>.</p>
            <blockquote>
            <p><strong>Application Profile:</strong> <a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/profiles/v1.1/lido-v1.1-profile-paintingandsculpture-v1.0.xsd"
            class="uri">https://lido-schema.org/profiles/v1.1/lido-v1.1-profile-paintingandsculpture-v1.0.xsd</a>
            <strong><em>Type:</em></strong> URI</p>
            </blockquote>
            <div class="sourceCode" id="cb9"><pre
            class="sourceCode xml"><code class="sourceCode xml"><span id="cb9-1"><a href="#cb9-1" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;<span class="kw">lido:applicationProfile</span></span>
<span id="cb9-2"><a href="#cb9-2" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a><span class="ot">    lido:type=</span><span class="st">&quot;http://terminology.lido-schema.org/lido00099&quot;</span>&gt;</span>
<span id="cb9-3"><a href="#cb9-3" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>    https://lido-schema.org/profiles/v1.1/lido-v1.1-profile-paintingandsculpture-v1.0.xsd</span>
<span id="cb9-4"><a href="#cb9-4" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1"></a>&lt;/<span class="kw">lido:applicationProfile</span>&gt;</span></code></pre></div>
            <h3 data-number="4.2.5" id="descriptive-metadata"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.2.5</span> Descriptive
            Metadata</h3>
            <p><strong>Element name:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#descriptiveMetadata">lido:descriptiveMetadata</a>&gt;</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> Descriptive metadata are intended
            to facilitate the discovery and identification of objects or
            works. They are most visible to end-users, guiding them
            through the physical or virtual shelves and enabling them to
            find what they need. Descriptive metadata comprise
            information on object properties, such as its type, title,
            physical features, or participation in an event. Note, that
            the use of &lt;lido:descriptiveMetadata&gt; provided with an
            xml:lang attribute is mandatory.</p>
            <figure>
            <img src="img/figure_descriptiveMetadata.png"
            alt="Figure 4: Descriptive Metadata and its immediate sub-elements" />
            <figcaption aria-hidden="true">Figure 4: Descriptive
            Metadata and its immediate sub-elements</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <h3 data-number="4.2.6" id="administrative-metadata"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.2.6</span> Administrative
            Metadata</h3>
            <p><strong>Element name:</strong> &lt;<a
            href="https://lido-schema.org/schema/latest/lido.html#administrativeMetadata">lido:administrativeMetadata</a>&gt;</p>
            <p><strong>Note:</strong> Administrative metadata are
            intended to facilitate the management of the data provided,
            particularly holding information about rights and
            restrictions on the access or use of the described object or
            work, the provided record or any supplied resource. Note,
            that the use of &lt;lido:administrativeMetadata&gt; provided
            with an xml:lang attribute is mandatory.</p>
            <figure>
            <img src="img/figure_administrativeMetadata.png"
            alt="Figure 5: Administrative Metadata and its immediate sub-elements" />
            <figcaption aria-hidden="true">Figure 5: Administrative
            Metadata and its immediate sub-elements</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <h2 data-number="4.3" id="element-nesting"><span
            class="header-section-number">4.3</span> Element
            nesting</h2>
            <p>Element nesting means that elements contain other
            elements that contain still other elements, and so forth. A
            nested structure of so called parent elements and child
            elements is a basic feature of XML. As with CDWA Lite, LIDO
            consists of wrapper and set elements that only serve to
            structure the data, and data elements for the actual data
 